course: everyday
lesson: 389
title: Boys who look at Maddie oddly
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Lately boys from both streets had started looking at Maddie «oddly». Kieran from her own street stared at her in class and went red as a postbox whenever she looked back. Maddie felt puzzled and a bit self-conscious; she checked her face for jam. Lily whispered, «I think he likes you.» Maddie said «ew!» and then giggled. At home her mum explained that people sometimes stare and go red when they like someone, and that it is perfectly normal.
explain: Then a boy from Station Road, the street that was supposed to be «at war» with hers, asked Maddie to the cinema. She was surprised and secretly pleased, and said she'd go if they bought popcorn for both streets. Kieran, meanwhile, wrote her a note and then lost his nerve and hid it. Lily found it under a desk and gave it back to him without reading a word. A crush can stay in a pocket, and that's fine.
explain: The most awkward moment was with Connor, the boy who once punched Maddie's nose because he was «all for equality». After he had said sorry, he admitted he had done it partly because he liked her and felt silly about it. Maddie was angry and confused all at once. «That's the worst way to like someone,» she told him. Liking someone is never an excuse to hurt them. They agreed to just be friends, and Maddie did not have to like him back.
explain: Jake told her she was «all right for a girl». «I'm all right for a person,» Maddie said, and Jake laughed and agreed. When her friends teased her about boys, she said «I'm ten, I like football», and they moved on. When Lily asked if she liked anyone, the two of them agreed to talk about crushes only when they wanted to, and never to push. And when Kieran showed off doing wheelies in front of her and went flying, Maddie laughed, but kindly, and helped him up.
fact: Crushes are normal, and they often make people act strangely: staring, blushing, showing off or going quiet.
fact: Being looked at «oddly» can simply mean someone likes you.
fact: You never have to like someone back.
fact: It is fine to not be interested in crushes yet.
fact: Liking someone is never an excuse to hurt them.
fact: Reading someone else's private note without asking is not respectful.
fact: A compliment with «for a girl» or «for a boy» attached is only half a compliment.
fact: Teasing friends about crushes makes them embarrassed and can push them into silly choices.
fact: Friends can talk about crushes, or not, whenever they choose.
fact: Showing off on a bike to impress someone often ends with a fall and a scraped elbow.
fact: Laughing kindly and then helping is different from laughing at someone.
exercise: How did Kieran act when Maddie looked back at him? | He went red | He stuck his tongue out | He threw a rubber at her | He pretended to be asleep
exercise: How did Maddie feel about being stared at? | Puzzled and a bit self-conscious | Proud and showing off to her friends | Angry enough to fight | Completely unbothered by it
exercise: What did Maddie's mum explain about the looks? | People sometimes look when they like someone | The boys secretly wanted to fight her | Boys always stare at girls who are tomboys | Maddie had something on her face
exercise: Why was the cinema invitation surprising? | The boy was from Station Road, the «enemy» street | The boy was in a much older class at another school | Maddie had never been to a cinema | The boy had punched her before
exercise: What did Maddie say to the cinema invitation? | She'd go if they bought popcorn for both streets | She'd go, but only if he fought Kieran first to prove it | She'd never speak to a Station Road boy | She'd go if he paid for her bike
exercise: What did Lily do with Kieran's hidden note? | Gave it back without reading it | Read it out loud to the whole class | Gave it straight to Maddie | Pinned it on the wall
exercise: Why was Lily right not to read the note? | It was Kieran's private note | Notes are always rude | She could not read his writing | Mrs Okafor was watching her
exercise: What did Connor admit after apologising? | He punched her partly because he liked her | He punched her because she had punched him first | He did it because his friends paid him | He didn't remember punching her
exercise: How did Maddie feel when Connor admitted that? | Angry and confused | Flattered and happy | Sorry for him and to blame | Nothing at all
exercise: What is true about Connor's reason for punching Maddie? | Liking someone is never an excuse to hurt them | It makes the punch less bad than it was | It means Maddie has to like him back now | It means it was only a joke
exercise: Jake said she was «all right for a girl». What did Maddie answer? | «I'm all right for a person» | «You're rubbish for a boy» | «Thanks, that's the best thing ever» | «Say that again and you're dead»
exercise: What is wrong with «all right for a girl»? | It's only half a compliment | It's a swear word | It means she is bad at football | It's what you say to babies
exercise: What did Maddie say when her friends teased her about boys? | «I'm ten, I like football» | «I'm going out with all of them» | «I'll punch whoever said it» | «I'm telling Mrs Okafor on you»
exercise: Kieran fell off his bike doing wheelies in front of Maddie. What did she do? | Laughed kindly, then helped him up | Laughed and filmed it on her phone | Walked away without a word | Told the whole street about it
exercise: Lily asked Maddie if she liked anyone. How did they decide to handle crushes? | Talk about them only when they wanted to | Tell each other every crush at once, straight away | Never be friends with boys again | Write all crushes in a shared diary
exercise: Which is the kindest way for Kieran to show he likes Maddie? | Be friendly and talk to her | Stare at her across the room | Do dangerous wheelies for her | Hide her football at break time
test: «kieran keeps staring at u and goin red lol». What's the likeliest reason? | He probably likes her | He wants to fight her | He's got a fever | He thinks she stole his pen
test: «u HAVE to go out with him, he asked u». Is that true? | No, you never have to like someone back | Yes, it's rude to say no to anyone | Yes, if he asked in front of people at school | Only if he's from your street
test: Connor: «I only did it cos I liked you». Maddie, fuming. What's true? | Liking her doesn't excuse hurting her | That makes it all right now, he liked her | Then she owes him a date | Then she should hit him back
test: Jake grins: «you're decent at footy for a girl». What's a smart reply? | «I'm decent for a person» | «Thanks, girls are usually bad» | «Shut it or I'll deck ya, mate» | «I'll stop playing then, see ya»
test: You find a note «to Maddie» under a desk. It's folded and says «private». What's right? | Give it back to whoever wrote it without reading it | Read it first to see if it's juicy, then give it back | Pass it round the class | Give it to Maddie and watch her face
test: «she's a tomboy, why would anyone fancy HER». What's true? | Anyone can be liked, tomboys too | Only girly girls get crushes | Tomboys never like anyone | Boys only stare at girls they hate
test: Kieran face-plants doing a wheelie right in front of her, everyone howls. What's kind? | Laugh with him, then help him up | Laugh loudest and point | Tell him he's a loser | Walk past as if nothing happened
test: Gang chanting «Maddie's got a boyfriend!» She's red and cross. Best thing for a friend to do? | Stop teasing and change the subject | Join in, it's only a bit of a laugh | Tell the boy that she likes him | Write it on the board
test: «r u gonna tell me who u like or what». Maddie doesn't want to. What's fair? | She can share when she chooses, or never | She must tell her best friend everything | She has to tell if anyone asks her twice | Lying is the only way out
test: Station Road lad asks her to the pictures. Her mates: «he's the ENEMY». What does the lesson say? | A crush can cross lines a street war draws | She must refuse him because of the war | She should challenge him to a fight first | The streets should vote on it
exam: Identify the conduct most commonly associated with a child who harbours a fondness for a classmate. | Staring, blushing or awkward conduct in the classmate's presence | Indifference towards the classmate at all times | Open hostility arising from genuine dislike | Formal written declarations of affection to the head teacher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an act of violence committed by a child towards a classmate for whom he professes affection? | The affection provides no excuse whatsoever for the violence | The affection substantially mitigates the wrong committed | The affection obliges the classmate to reciprocate | The affection renders the act a form of play
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the obligations of a child who is the object of another child's fondness. | She is under no obligation to return the fondness | She is obliged to return the fondness if it is declared | She is obliged to declare her own preferences when asked | She is obliged to accept any invitation extended to her
exam: It is given that a pupil discovers a private note addressed by one classmate to another. State the conduct most consistent with respect for privacy. | Its return to the author unread | Its perusal prior to delivery | Its circulation among the class | Its submission to the recipient in public
